# Docker reads the .dockerignore next to the *build context*, not next to the
# Dockerfile. The backend image is built with the repo root as context (it needs
# the workspace lockfile), so this file is the one that takes effect. A
# .dockerignore inside packages/backend would be read by nothing.

node_modules
**/node_modules

.git
.gitignore

# Secrets. .env.local is the one that actually exists locally; the rest are
# listed so a future one cannot be added and quietly baked into a layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# Build output — regenerated inside the image, and copying a host build in
# would ship whatever architecture the developer's laptop happens to be.
**/.next
**/dist
**/build

# Tests and fixtures: not needed at runtime, and they enlarge the layer that
# gets rebuilt most often.
**/__tests__
**/*.test.ts
**/*.test.tsx
**/vitest.config.mts

# Local noise
**/*.log
**/.DS_Store
**/coverage
**/.vscode
**/.idea
docker-compose*.yml
